// test_bridge_v1_raisedwater_bigjam_mixed_level3_DEBUG_crashrepro_floaterlog_gas.prm
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THIRD parallel run (separate mirror checkout, mt-dennys) reproducing job 4166006's EXACT
// configuration (Z=74 spawn height, ORIGINAL/untripled release timing, same exact
// freshlyActivatedFloaterUIDs/ComputeContacts overlap check) -- the only change is
// ModelParameters.logFloaterHistory turned ON (unfiltered), to get the fine-grained per-sub-cycle
// force/torque/velocity breakdown that job 4166006 didn't capture. Expected to reproduce the same
// abort (uid=1441151880758558745 vs uid=576460752303423513, ~step 8001) -- the floater-history CSV
// this time should show what's actually happening to uid513 in the steps leading up to it.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FOURTH debug attempt for the "flying floater" chain traced from job 4164460. Root cause
// (jobs 4165658/4165779): a floater released directly on top of an already-active, barely-drifted
// neighbour sharing its spawn point. assertNoOverlapAtRelease() (SetupStages.h) now hard-aborts
// -- with both floaters' uid/shapeType/pos/interactionRadius/overlap in the message -- if this
// happens, using a conservative bounding-sphere (interaction-radius sum) test.
//
// Job 4165898 (settlingSteps tripled, 3000/9000-step gaps -> 9000/27000) aborted at runtime 621s
// on a box-box pair (uid519 vs uid507, center distance 9.31, combined bounding radius 12.53) that
// is very likely a FALSE POSITIVE of the bounding-sphere test: working out each box's actual
// axis-aligned extent (halfExtents <6,1,1.5>, both apparently still near-unrotated) puts them
// Y:[10,12] vs Y:[3.88,5.88] -- a clean 4.12 LU separating gap the isotropic bounding-sphere check
// can't see, since it's dominated by the box's long (X=6) half-extent even though the true
// footprint is thin in Y/Z. Not yet fixed (a real per-shape-pair test, e.g. reusing
// SolidCylinderContactDetection/GJK-EPA directly, would close this) -- flagged here as a known
// limitation of THIS check specifically, to keep in mind for any future firing.
//
// UPDATE: assertNoOverlapAtRelease()'s bounding-sphere test produced TWO false positives in a row
// (jobs 4165898, 4165931 -- both on the same box uid=576460752303423519 against a different box
// whose actual axis-aligned Y-extent cleanly separated them by 4 LU, invisible to an isotropic
// sphere test dominated by the box's long X half-extent). Replaced with a better mechanism instead
// of a tighter approximation: releaseSettledFloatingSpheres() now just inserts the released UID
// into simObjs->freshlyActivatedFloaterUIDs, and ComputeContacts (ParticleKernels.h) -- which the
// floater falls into naturally on the very next doDEMSubCycles() call, using the EXACT same GJK/
// EPA/analytical detection real physics uses -- hard-aborts if it finds a genuine contact involving
// that UID. No approximation, no false positives, no duplicated geometry code; the set is cleared
// every step so it only ever covers the one main step right after release.
//
// Job 4166006 (release timing reverted to ORIGINAL/untripled, Z=74) confirmed a REAL overlap with
// the new exact check: box uid=1441151880758558745 (spawn <22,24,74>) vs box uid=576460752303423513
// (drifted from spawn <15,17,74>, a DIFFERENT/adjacent aim point, to <15.93,18.42,71.70> over 1000
// steps) -- penetration=-0.479, normal=(0,1,0). Notably this pair's spawn FOOTPRINTS don't even
// overlap at t=0 (clean 5 LU Y gap between the two aim points); it was uid513's own drift + its own
// 12-LU box length that closed the gap. uid513 was still 6.6 LU above the water line (Z=71.70 vs
// ~65.12) when this happened -- pure dry drift, not water interaction.
//
// THIS run: spawn Z raised again, 74 -> 86 (all 162 entries) -- water clearance 8.88 -> 20.88 LU,
// now exceeding even level1's own 13.67 LU margin. Kept inside the Z=[75,90) block (cellsPerBlock
// Z=15) with margin on both the 75 boundary and the domain ceiling (90 - sphere radius 2.581 =
// 87.42 is the hard limit; 86 clears it by 1.42). Release timing left at the ORIGINAL/untripled
// schedule. timesteps=100000.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// RAISED-WATER VARIANT of test_bridge_v1_lowwater_bigjam_mixed_gas.prm (the confirmed-clean
// mixed-shape big-jam scenario, job 4142357 -- 54 box + 54 sphere + 54 cylinder floaters, arches
// as real mesa_pd objects). Every parameter is verbatim from that file EXCEPT liquidHeightFactor:
// 2.0 (absoluteLiquidHeight~49.1, below the springing line) -> 4.0 (absoluteLiquidHeight~65.12),
// the deepest water level ever run against this bridge geometry: within ~1 LU of the CENTRAL arch's own crown (66) while still ~11 LU clear of the deck top (76), so it stays a genuine arch-interaction test rather than sliding into deck-overtopping territory (see test_bridge_v1_overtop_gas.prm's own liquidHeightFactor=5.9 for that different regime).
//
// This is one of THREE water-level variants (level1=2.9/~56.33, level2=3.2/~58.73,
// level3=4.0/~65.12) submitted together, one per repo checkout (main mt-dennys, mt-dennys-mirror,
// mt-dennys-mirror2) so all three run concurrently instead of queueing behind each other --
// specifically to stress-test the new arch/tunnel containment code (radial pre-gate + envelope
// clamp, ParticleKernels.h) across a range of genuine submersion depths, since the original
// low-water run's floaters mostly passed underneath the arches in open water and only grazed the
// curved ceiling occasionally (600 [ARCH-ENVELOP] firings over the whole run, job 4142357).
// domainSize/timesteps/numSubCycles/piers/arches/ModelParameters/floater mix are all unchanged --
// see the base file's own header, and test_bridge_v1_lowwater_bigjam_gas.prm before it, for the
// full derivation chain.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

BlockForestParameters
{
   cellsPerBlock                 < 32, 20, 15 >;
   periodicity                   < 1, 0, 0 >;
   loadSnapshot                  false;
   storeSnapshot                 true;
   snapshotFrequency             10000;
   snapshotBaseFolder            snapshots;
}

DomainParameters
{
   domainSize         <128, 60, 90>;
   wavePeriods        1;
   liquidHeightFactor 4.0;  // raised-water variant (level3) -- see this file's own header; absoluteLiquidHeight~65.12
   floorHeightFactor  4.1393;
   initialAmplitude   0;
}

PIDParameters
{
   targetMeanVelocityMagnitude 0.01;
   proportionalGain            2e-4;
   derivativeGain              1e-6;
   integralGain                2e-4;
   maxRamp                     1e-4;
   minActuatingVariable        0;
   maxActuatingVariable        1e-3;
}

PhysicsParameters
{
   enableWetting     false;
   timesteps         100000;
   Re                3100;
   Fr                1.31;
   We                15.6188;
   useHardcodedUnits true;
   // no gravityOverride -- dynamically computed gravity
}

ParticleParameters
{
   inFileName spheres_out.dat;
   bedCopiesInX 1;
   bedCopiesInY 1;
   densityRatio 2.55;
   fixingHeightFactor 1.5;
   frictionCoefficient 0.5;
   restitutionCoefficient 0.3; // wet/waterlogged wood-wood -- these are floating logs, soaked, not dry timber
   numSubCycles 40; // 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> 40. 20 got job 4139553 all the way to a clean 200000-step
                     // finish (with useAnalyticalContactDetection also on) -- no repeat of the
                     // NaN-quaternion crash from job 4139454. 30 was for the SEPARATE "flying
                     // cuboid" events found by inspecting that clean run: elongated (12 LU) box
                     // floaters tumbling on arrival at the bridge entrance can swing a far corner
                     // several LU into an arch tunnel wall within a single sub-step even though
                     // their centre is nowhere close. This further bump (job 4140767 postmortem)
                     // is for a THIRD, distinct mechanism: two floating boxes colliding with each
                     // other right at pier 1's location while one of them simultaneously envelops
                     // the pillar's thin cylinder -- confirmed via the floater-history CSV
                     // (detectionPath="pillar exterior (Box, ENVELOP-clamped)") that the SAME
                     // clamped overlap recurred unchanged across 5 consecutive sub-cycles instead
                     // of separating, compounding into a ~500-magnitude force. Finer sub-cycling
                     // means the box travels less between force recomputations, giving the
                     // (now also tightened, see ParticleKernels.h maxOverlap 1.5->1.0) pillar
                     // spring more chances to actually separate the geometry instead of re-firing
                     // at the same clamped depth.
   useLubricationCorrection true;
   useNoSlipParticles false;
   useParticleBed false;
}

// DEM bodies matching the BodyBC piers exactly (BodyBC is LBM-only -- floaters need a real mesa_pd
// body to collide with, and nothing else creates one). 4 cylinders (the piers' round end-caps,
// radius 5) + 2 boxes (the connecting box between them, halfExtents = half of X=61-67/Y=10-20-or-
// 40-50/Z=0-76). center Z is cosmetic (CylindricalBoundary is infinite along its axis).
CylinderPillars
{
   CylinderPillar { center < 61, 15, 38 >; radius 5; }
   CylinderPillar { center < 67, 15, 38 >; radius 5; }
   CylinderPillar { center < 61, 45, 38 >; radius 5; }
   CylinderPillar { center < 67, 45, 38 >; radius 5; }
}

Pillars
{
   Pillar { center < 64, 15, 38 >; halfExtents < 3, 5, 38 >; }
   Pillar { center < 64, 45, 38 >; halfExtents < 3, 5, 38 >; }
}

// One entry per arch, matching the BodyBC arch cylinders below exactly (center Y/Z, radius).
// xMin/xMax DELIBERATELY set to the piers' own straight-box span (61..67). Not mesa_pd bodies --
// consumed directly by ParticleKernels.h's ComputeTunnelContacts, which only engages the UPPER
// semicircle (Z >= center[2] = the springing line, 56) -- confirmed clean (0 spurious contacts,
// 100% of real contacts above the springing line) on both the lowwater run (job 4134882) and a
// dedicated raised-water run built specifically to force genuine arch interaction (job 4135755).
// deckTopZ 76 added (job investigation into "does the code model the arch's front/back face
// and top?"): each ArchTunnel now also creates a real deck-fill solid box from its own crown
// (center.z+radius) up to the deck top (matching the BodyBC arch boxes' own max Z, 76 below) --
// see ArchTunnelParams' own comment (SimulationParams.h) and SetupStages.h's arch-body creation
// loop for the full derivation. yMin/yMax deliberately left at their defaults (center.y -+
// radius): this bridge's own BodyBC arch boxes have Y-span EXACTLY equal to each arch's own
// diameter (confirmed directly: left/right 10 = 2*5, central 20 = 2*10), so there is no separate
// flat haunch wall to add beside the curve -- the curve itself is the front/back face below the
// crown, already handled by the existing curved containment body.
ArchTunnels
{
   ArchTunnel { center < 0, 5, 56 >;  radius 5;  xMin 61; xMax 67; deckTopZ 76; }  // left arch
   ArchTunnel { center < 0, 30, 56 >; radius 10; xMin 61; xMax 67; deckTopZ 76; }  // central arch
   ArchTunnel { center < 0, 55, 56 >; radius 5;  xMin 61; xMax 67; deckTopZ 76; }  // right arch
}

// Log-jam debris: 9 upstream aim points (2 rows: X=15/X=22), 6 stacked "logs" per point per
// shape (18 per point total: 6 box + 6 sphere + 6 cylinder), staggered release (startInactive +
// settlingSteps) in 3000-step waves, wave index mod 3 cycling shape -- see this file's own header
// for the full rationale (equal-volume sizing, why this exercises every mixed-shape contact pair).
FloatingBoxes
{
